v5.3.3
Radon Player 5.3.3: The DJ checked for repeats and wrong numbers, and the right EQ when the music moves
- **The DJ never brings a song back too soon (the phone and the PC):** every set it picks ahead is checked again at the moment it's used, against what it played and what's still to come. Sets picked while the queue changed (you asked for something, switched it up, added a song of your own, skipped as it began) could share songs with the set before or after. A stream and your own file of one song no longer both land in a set, an ask can't return the song already playing or one you queued, a first set made ahead isn't used after its songs were played, and a song you queued yourself during the DJ's run is remembered like the DJ's own.
- **The DJ's numbers are the song's own:** “your 100th play of…”, “you've played it N times” and the top ten remarks go by the song on its album, as Most played does. With two songs of one name (the two “Yesterday”s), the lesser one was credited with the other's plays and called your most played.
- **A milestone isn't lost to a skip:** “this is your 100th play” was marked as told when it was prepared, a song ahead; it's marked when it is said.
- **Wording put right:** “on 8 of the last seven days” can't happen (the last seven calendar days are counted), “1 days ago” is “a day ago”, an artist spotlight no longer says “0 plays from you”, and a mood line no longer says “0 calm songs this month”.
- **Artists named whole:** “by Tyler”, “your most played artist this month is Tyler” and “listening to Earth since…” are Tyler, The Creator and Earth, Wind & Fire (the names that stay whole).
- **“Play the 80s” plays your songs from the 80s:** a decade asked for goes by the year in your files first, as the DJ's own decade sets do.
- **The right EQ the moment the music moves (the phone):** with your headphones and the car both connected, the app went by the first Bluetooth device in Android's list, whichever was playing. So the car could play with the headphones' EQ until the headphones dropped out of range down the road, when the car's EQ (and its volume) suddenly came in. It now goes by the device the music really plays on, and looks every few seconds while music plays, so the output's own EQ and default preset come in as soon as the sound moves. Android 13 and up can tell which of two Bluetooth devices is playing; older phones keep the old behaviour.
- **“This month” is the calendar month, everywhere the DJ says it:** your most played artist, song and mood of the month, an artist found this month, and the month's mood compared with last month's all go by the calendar month. Some went by the last thirty days, so early in a month the DJ quoted last month's plays as this month's.
- **The DJ's counts are up to the minute:** a play counted in the app since its numbers were last added up is added on top, so “42 times” is what the row shows, and a milestone isn't missed because the play before it wasn't counted yet.
- **What you hid from the stats stays out of the DJ's ranks:** “number four in your top ten” is the fourth on your screen, and a hidden artist or song is never named as your most played.
- **Last.fm first, the app's own account without it:** with Last.fm connected the DJ counts by Last.fm (and your imports). Without Last.fm it counts the plays made in the app with your imports, as Most played does; they had been left out whenever there was an import.
- **“Found N days ago, played M times since” is true:** M is the plays since that first one. It was every play the song ever had, so a song with older plays from an import could be “found 5 days ago, played 300 times since”. A song with older plays that have no date isn't said to have been found or first played at any time.
